WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.206
The ocean is one of the largest buffers against
climate change,

00:00:04.286 --> 00:00:08.015
soaking up a big fraction of the carbon dioxide we emit.

00:00:08.095 --> 00:00:11.824
But our best official accounting of how much it absorbs,

00:00:11.904 --> 00:00:16.654
the annual Global Carbon Budget, always trails
reality by about a year.

00:00:16.734 --> 00:00:19.511
That means when the ocean's uptake shifts,

00:00:19.591 --> 00:00:22.912
we simply cannot see it until long after the fact.

00:00:22.992 --> 00:00:27.946
This lag is baked in: the biogeochemical models are
computationally heavy,

00:00:28.026 --> 00:00:34.000
and the surface-ocean observations they rely on are
themselves delayed by roughly a year.
